      "commentary": "Both his rooks now point at my e2 knight. Counting: it's attacked twice and defended by the e1 rook and the g3 knight, so a full exchange on e2 actually favours me, but I don't want to be recapturing with the knight while his queen checks on h2 and h1. Kf1 keeps everything simple: the king itself recaptures on e2, there is no back-rank mate, and his queen's diagonal to f2 and e1 is blocked by my own g3 knight. Checked square by square this time. Next: if he plays ...Qh2 I take on f5 and the g4 knight is loose; ...Rxg3 still fails to Qxg3; ...Re4 is met by Qd2; and ...Rf3+ gxf3 Qh3+ Kg1 Qxg3+ runs into Qxg4 at the end.",
